Building the hexagon_loadlinux firmware
========================================

``pc-bios/hexagon_loadlinux_v81`` is the default firmware for the hexagon
``virt`` machine.  It is the "loadlinux" bootloader from the H2 hypervisor
project: it initializes the H2 hypervisor and jumps to a Linux kernel loaded
at physical address 0xa0000000, passing the device tree blob address in
r1:0.

It is built for three Hexagon architecture revisions -- v68, v73 and v81 --
producing ``pc-bios/hexagon_loadlinux_v68``, ``pc-bios/hexagon_loadlinux_v73``
and ``pc-bios/hexagon_loadlinux_v81``.  Select a non-default variant with
``-bios hexagon_loadlinux_v68`` (or ``_v73``).

It is built from the ``roms/hexagon-hypervisor`` submodule with the Hexagon
SDK toolchain.  From the top of that submodule, build H2 itself, then the
loadlinux bootloader that links against it, for each ARCHV in turn:

  make USE_PKW=0 ARCHV=<68|73|81> TARGET=opt NULL_ANGEL_TRAP=1
  make -C linux USE_PKW=0 ARCHV=<68|73|81> NO_LOAD=1 NULL_ANGEL_TRAP=1 \
      LINUX_LINK_ADDR=0xa0000000 loadlinux

To rebuild them, ensure ``hexagon-clang`` and ``hexagon-strip`` (from the
`Hexagon SDK <https://softwarecenter.qualcomm.com/catalog/item/Hexagon_SDK>`_)
are on your PATH, then from the top of the QEMU tree run::

  make -C roms hexagon-loadlinux

This updates ``pc-bios/hexagon_loadlinux_v68``, ``pc-bios/hexagon_loadlinux_v73``
and ``pc-bios/hexagon_loadlinux_v81`` in place.  Each image is stripped
of debug info but keeps its ELF program headers so QEMU can load it with
load_elf().  A single variant can be rebuilt with, e.g.,
``make -C roms hexagon-loadlinux-v68``.
